bls12_381 — provenance note
============================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`adaptor`/`musig2`/`slhdsa`/`falcon` modules — self-contained crypto
modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

bls12_381 — clean-room implementation of the BLS12-381 pairing-friendly
elliptic curve, from public specifications and design references (no
third-party SOURCE was ported into this module):

1. **draft-irtf-cfrg-pairing-friendly-curves** (IETF CFRG draft) — the
   BLS12-381 parameter family definition (the defining polynomials
   `p(z)`, `r(z)`, `t(z)` for the BLS12 curve family and the specific
   parameter `z = -0xd201000000010000` that BLS12-381 instantiates them
   with). A public IETF draft/specification — not a copyrightable work
   (merger doctrine, `CONVENTIONS.md` §5) — cited here for completeness,
   not because attribution is a license condition.
2. **The ZCash/IETF BLS12-381 point-serialization convention**
   (originally specified by the ZCash project for BLS12-381 pairing
   output/point encoding; the same convention was later carried into
   `draft-irtf-cfrg-pairing-friendly-curves`' own serialization
   appendix, `hash_to_curve`'s companion specs, and is what every
   reference implementation cited below implements): the flag-bit
   layout (`compression`/`infinity`/`sort` in the top 3 bits of the
   first serialized byte), the compressed (48/96-byte) and uncompressed
   (96/192-byte) point formats for `G1`/`G2`, and — the easy-to-miss
   detail this module's `fp2.zig` documents prominently — that an `Fp2`
   element `c0 + c1*u` serializes as `c1 || c0` (HIGH component first),
   not `c0 || c1`.
3. **EIP-2537** (Ethereum "Precompile for BLS12-381 curve operations") —
   consulted for a SEPARATE, simpler wire convention (fixed-width
   64/128-byte big-endian field-element padding, no flag bits) used by
   that precompile — NOT the encoding this module implements (this
   module follows convention (2) above throughout) — cited here only
   because it independently corroborates the `p`/`r`/generator constant
   VALUES this module embeds (cross-referenced, not used as the
   authoritative source for any of them).
4. **`ethereum/py_ecc`** (MIT license,
   https://github.com/ethereum/py_ecc) — a DESIGN/FACT reference, not
   ported source (`CONVENTIONS.md` §5): two of its files —
   `py_ecc/optimized_bls12_381/optimized_curve.py` and
   `py_ecc/bls12_381/bls12_381_curve.py` — were fetched (2026-07-14) and
   found to AGREE byte-for-byte on `field_modulus`, `curve_order`, the
   `G1`/`G2` curve constant `b`/`b2`, and the `G1`/`G2` generator point
   coordinates. Those coordinate VALUES (decimal integers in the
   Python source) were converted to hex and embedded in this module's
   `g1.zig`/`g2.zig` as plain numeric constants — no Python (or any
   other) SOURCE CODE, algorithm, or API shape was copied; only the
   numeric FACTS (which are not independently copyrightable — a
   mathematical constant is not an expression) were taken, and even
   those were independently re-verified rather than trusted (see
   "Verification performed" below).
5. **`zkcrypto/bls12_381`** (Rust, MIT/Apache-2.0,
   https://github.com/zkcrypto/bls12_381) and **`supranational/blst`**
   (C/assembly, Apache-2.0, https://github.com/supranational/blst) —
   named as the two most widely-deployed BLS12-381 reference
   implementations for the FIELD-ARITHMETIC CONSTRUCTIONS this module
   uses (`Fp2`'s Karatsuba 3-multiplication form, `Fp6`/`Fp12`'s
   Devegili-et-al.-style tower multiplication/squaring/inversion, the
   complex-method `Fp2.sqrt`). NEITHER repository's source code was
   read line-by-line during EITHER pass (scaffold or crypto-core): the
   crypto-core pass implemented every formula from the ACADEMIC
   references below ((6), (8), (9)) and validated them against
   independently-computed vectors (see "Verification performed"); the
   zkcrypto constant names mentioned in two test comments
   (`FROBENIUS_COEFF_*`) refer to published NUMERIC values compared as
   numbers, not to consulted code.
6. **Devegili, Ó hÉigeartaigh, Scott, Dahab, "Multiplication and
   Squaring on Pairing-Friendly Fields"** (Cryptology ePrint 2006/471)
   — the standard reference for `Fp6`/`Fp12` Karatsuba-style tower
   arithmetic formulas, cited directly in `fp6.zig`/`fp12.zig`'s doc
   comments. A public academic paper — cited for attribution courtesy,
   not a license requirement.
7. **Bowe, "Faster Subgroup Checks for BLS12-381"** (Cryptology ePrint
   2019/814) — the untwist-Frobenius-twist subgroup-check/cofactor-
   clearing technique named (not implemented) in `g1.zig`/`g2.zig`'s
   `subgroupCheck`/`clearCofactor` doc comments as the eventual
   fast-path alternative to the simple `scalarMul`-by-order/cofactor
   forms actually implemented (the always-correct first version).
8. **Adj & Rodriguez-Henriquez, "Square root computation over even
   extension fields"** (Cryptology ePrint 2012/685, Algorithm 9) — the
   "complex method" square root over `Fp2` for `p ≡ 3 (mod 4)` base
   fields, implemented in `fp2.zig`'s `sqrt`. A public academic paper —
   cited for attribution courtesy.
9. **Bernstein & Lange, "Explicit-Formulas Database"** (EFD,
   https://hyperelliptic.org/EFD/) — the `add-2007-bl` Jacobian
   addition and `dbl-2009-l` doubling formulas (`shortw/jacobian-0`)
   implemented in `g1.zig`/`g2.zig`. A public formula catalogue —
   cited for attribution courtesy.

10. **Miller, "Short Programs for Functions on Curves"** (unpublished
    manuscript, 1986) — the original Miller-loop construction: the
    double-and-add walk that accumulates a rational function's evaluation
    along a scalar multiple of a curve point, the basis of every pairing
    algorithm since. Cited in `pairing.zig`'s module doc comment and
    `multiMillerLoop`'s doc comment.
11. **Vercauteren, "Optimal Pairings"** (IEEE Transactions on Information
    Theory 56(1), 2010) — the "optimal ate" pairing variant this module's
    Part 2 names and is scaffolded around: for the BLS12 curve family,
    the shortest known Miller-loop length for the embedding degree
    `k=12`, keyed to the low-Hamming-weight seed
    `x = -0xd201000000010000`. A public academic paper — cited for
    attribution courtesy.
12. **Costello, Lange, Naehrig, "Faster Pairing Computations on Curves
    with High-Degree Twists"** (PKC 2010) — the D-type-twist line-
    evaluation/untwisting formulas `multiMillerLoop`'s doc comment points
    Fable at (BLS12-381's `G2` sextic twist is D-type, confirmed by
    `g2.zig`'s own `b' = 4(1+u)` curve constant and the `u²=-1`
    non-residue convention — using the M-type untwisting instead is a
    well-known BLS12-381 implementation footgun). A public academic
    paper — cited for attribution courtesy.
13. **Scott, Benger, Charlemagne, Perez, Kachisa, "On the Final
    Exponentiation for Calculating Pairings on Ordinary Elliptic
    Curves"** (Cryptology ePrint 2008/490) — the standard easy-part/
    hard-part split `pairing.zig`'s `finalExponentiation` implements:
    the easy part (`f^((p^6-1)(p^2+1))`) is a fixed composition of
    `Fp12` primitives (see `finalExpEasyPart`'s doc comment for the
    exact identity); the hard part is entry 17's chain.
14. **Fuentes-Castañeda, Knapp, Rodríguez-Henríquez, "Faster Hashing to
    G2"** (Cryptology ePrint 2011/465) — the BLS12-family hard-part
    addition chain the scaffold originally pointed at. EVALUATED and
    NOT used: its chain computes `f^(3d)` (`d = (p^4-p^2+1)/r`) — a
    fixed cube of the canonical pairing, bilinearity-equivalent but not
    byte-equal to the IETF draft's `e(G1,G2)` test vector (both facts
    verified numerically for this exact seed before deciding) — see
    entry 17 for the exact-`d` identity implemented instead. A public
    academic paper — cited for attribution courtesy.
15. **`zkcrypto/bls12_381`/`supranational/blst`** (already named in
    entry 5 above, same license/non-read-source status) — named in
    `pairing.zig` as the most widely deployed implementations sharing
    this module's Miller-loop conventions (negative-seed conjugation,
    D-type line evaluation). Their source remains UNREAD; the Part-2
    crypto-core pass validated its own construction against the IETF
    draft's official test vector and py_ecc instead (see "Verification
    performed (Part-2 crypto-core pass)" below).
16. **`ethereum/py_ecc`** (already named in entry 4 above, same
    MIT/design-reference status) — the `e(G1,G2)` KAT CROSS-CHECK
    source: `py_ecc.bls12_381.pairing` (version 8.0.0, installed from
    PyPI 2026-07-14) was run to compute `e(G1,G2)` independently; its
    output equals the IETF draft's canonical vector up to exactly one
    conjugation (py_ecc's Miller loop walks `|x|` without the
    negative-seed adjustment — determined by READING
    `bls12_381_pairing.py`, which is MIT-licensed and was consulted
    here as a FACT source about py_ecc's own convention, not as a
    design source for this module's already-written loop). The
    basis conversion used for the comparison (py_ecc's flat
    `Fp[w]/(w^12-2w^6+2)` -> this module's tower, via `u = w^6-1`) was
    validated as a ring isomorphism on random products before use.
17. **Hayashida, Hayasaka, Teruya, "Efficient Final Exponentiation via
    Cyclotomic Structure for Pairings over Families of Elliptic
    Curves"** (Cryptology ePrint 2020/875) — the EXACT-`d` hard-part
    identity `finalExpHardPart` implements (BLS12 case):
    `d = (p^4-p^2+1)/r = k*(x+p)*(x^2+p^2-1) + 1`, `k = (x-1)^2/3`.
    Re-verified numerically as an exact integer identity for this
    module's seed before implementation; `k` is comptime-derived from
    the seed in code (no transcribed constant). A public academic
    paper — cited for attribution courtesy.
18. **draft-irtf-cfrg-pairing-friendly-curves-11, Appendix B** — the
    PRIMARY `e(G1,G2)` KAT source: the official BLS12-381 optimal-ate
    test vector (`e_0..e_11`), fetched 2026-07-14 from ietf.org and
    mechanically reordered into this module's tower/wire layout (the
    draft's input points were confirmed to be this module's own
    generators). Matches this module's pairing output byte-for-byte.
    Same not-copyrightable-parameter status as entry 1.

19. **RFC 9380** ("Hashing to Elliptic Curves", IETF CFRG/CFRG-adjacent
    RFC) — the hash-to-curve specification Part 3 (`hash_to_curve.zig`,
    SCAFFOLDED 2026-07-14) implements: `expand_message_xmd` (§5.3.1),
    `hash_to_field` (§5.2), the Simplified SWU map for `AB == 0` curves
    (§6.6.2/§6.6.3), and the two BLS12-381 suite definitions
    `BLS12381G1_XMD:SHA-256_SSWU_RO_`/`BLS12381G2_XMD:SHA-256_SSWU_RO_`
    (§8.8.1/§8.8.2, including the `Z`/`A'`/`B'` SSWU parameters for both
    `G1`'s and `G2`'s isogenous curves, embedded directly in
    `hash_to_curve.zig` as small cited constants — see entry 20). A
    public IETF RFC — not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine, same
    status as entry 1's pairing-friendly-curves draft), cited here for
    completeness. Fetched as raw text
    (`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9380.txt`, 2026-07-14) — every
    numeric constant and test vector this pass embeds was extracted
    programmatically from that raw text (not retyped from a rendered
    page, and not trusted from memory) and cross-checked for the
    expected byte length before being committed to source; see
    "Verification performed (Part-3 scaffold pass)" below.
20. **RFC 9380 Appendix E.2/E.3** (the 11-isogeny map for `G1`, the
    3-isogeny map for `G2`) — the rational-map coefficient tables
    `isogenyMap11`/`isogenyMap3` (`hash_to_curve.zig`) embed: 53 `Fp`
    constants (E.2: `x_num` 12, monic `x_den` 10, `y_num` 16, monic
    `y_den` 15) and 13 `Fp2` constants (E.3: 4 + 2 + 4 + 3). SOURCED by
    the Part-3 crypto-core pass (2026-07-14) programmatically from the
    RFC's raw text — a scratch Python parser split the `k_(i,j) = ...`
    entries of the fetched `rfc9380.txt` (entry 19's copy), joined the
    multi-line hex continuations mechanically, range-checked every
    value against `p`, and emitted the Zig table literals directly (no
    hand retyping of any value at any point). Verified independently —
    see "Verification performed (Part-3 crypto-core pass)" below. Same
    not-copyrightable-parameter status as entry 19.
21. **RFC 9380 §7 + §8.8.1/§8.8.2 `h_eff`** — the suite-pinned
    `clear_cofactor` scalars `hash_to_curve.zig` embeds
    (`g1_h_eff_bytes` = `0xd201000000010001`, `g2_h_eff_bytes` = the
    636-bit §8.8.2 value), both cited from the RFC's raw text. §7
    defines `clear_cofactor(P) := h_eff * P` and — for suites whose
    `h_eff` comes from a fast clearing method, which both BLS12-381
    suites' do (Scott [WB19] §5 for `G1`, Budroni-Pintore [BP17] §4.1
    for `G2`, per the RFC's own suite notes) — states that plain
    multiplication by the cofactor `h` "does not generally give the
    same result ... and MUST NOT be used". This CORRECTED the
    scaffold's stated plan to reuse `g1`/`g2.Jacobian.clearCofactor`
    (multiply-by-true-`h`) for RFC 9380's `clear_cofactor`; see the
    verification section below for the empirical confirmation that the
    two differ.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
`std.crypto.ff` (`Modulus`/`Fe`/`Uint` — MIT, part of Zig itself) is used
via its public API for all Montgomery modular arithmetic underneath `Fp`/
`Fr`; no new big-integer or modular-arithmetic primitive is implemented
by this module.

Verification performed
-----------------------

Every numeric constant this scaffold embeds was INDEPENDENTLY
RE-DERIVED or cross-checked, not merely transcribed from a single
source, before being committed to source:

- **`p`, `r`**: both re-derived directly from the BLS12-381 defining
  parameter `z = -0xd201000000010000` via the pairing-friendly-curves
  family formulas `r(z) = z^4 - z^2 + 1` and `p(z) = (z-1)^2 * (z^4 -
  z^2 + 1) / 3 + z`, and confirmed to equal the values fetched from
  source (4) above, byte-for-byte.
- **`G1`/`G2` generator coordinates**: fetched from source (4)'s TWO
  independent files (which agreed with each other), then independently
  confirmed ON-CURVE by direct modular arithmetic — `y^2 == x^3 + 4 (mod
  p)` for `G1`, and `y^2 == x^3 + 4(1+u) (mod p)` in `Fp2` (using the
  `u^2 = -1` non-residue convention this module documents throughout)
  for `G2` — rather than trusted on citation alone.
- **`G1`/`G2` compressed/uncompressed serialization bytes** (the KAT
  vectors embedded in `g1.zig`/`g2.zig`'s tests): computed FROM the
  above independently-verified generator coordinates by direct,
  from-scratch application of the documented ZCash/IETF flag-bit
  encoding rule (source (2) above) — NOT copied from any third-party
  test suite. The resulting compressed `G1` generator bytes
  (`97f1d3a7...`) match the well-known publicly-cited value for this
  constant, corroborating both the generator coordinates and the
  encoding-rule implementation independently.
- **`h1` (`G1` cofactor)**: re-derived from `z` via `#E(Fp) = p + 1 -
  (z+1)`, `h1 = #E(Fp) / r`, and confirmed the division is EXACT (zero
  remainder) — not transcribed from any single source.
- **`h2` (`G2` cofactor)** — **CORRECTED during the crypto-core pass
  (2026-07-14): the scaffold's original derivation was WRONG.** The
  scaffold computed `p^2 + 1 - t2` (`t2 = t^2 - 2p`, `t = z+1`) — but
  that is the order of `E(Fp2)` (the ORIGINAL curve over `Fp2`), not of
  the SEXTIC twist `E'` that `g2.zig` implements. `#E(Fp2)` is also
  divisible by `r` (it contains `E(Fp) ⊇ G1`), so the scaffold's
  exact-division cross-check passed despite describing the wrong
  curve; the resulting value differed from the true `h2` in its last
  16 bytes (`...4f83786ecdc4edc75b1ce38e1c72e38f` instead of
  `...1616ec6e786f0c70cf1c38e31c7238e5`). The corrected value was
  re-derived from the CM equation for the sextic twists —
  `f^2 = (4p^2 - t2^2)/3`, `#E'(Fp2) = p^2 + 1 - (t2 - 3f)/2`
  (the `(t2 + 3f)/2` sign gives the other sextic twist, whose order is
  NOT divisible by `r` — cross-checked), `h2 = #E'(Fp2)/r` EXACT — and
  verified two more independent ways: (a) it matches the h2 published
  across BLS12-381 implementations (kilic/bls12-381 `bls12_381.go`,
  paulmillr/noble-bls12-381 `math.ts`, and the
  pairing-friendly-curves draft's parameter listing); (b) EMPIRICALLY,
  for several independently-constructed non-subgroup points `P` on
  `E'(Fp2)`, `[#E']P == O` and `[r]([h2]P) == O` hold with the
  corrected value and BOTH FAIL with the scaffold's old value
  (Lagrange's theorem rules the old value out as a multiple of any
  point order). `g2.zig`'s tests enforce the behavioral consequence
  (`clearCofactor` of a non-subgroup point passes `subgroupCheck`).

All of the above re-derivation was performed with an independent
Python scratch computation (not part of this repository, not itself a
design reference — see `bip340`/`adaptor`'s precedent for this same
"compute independently, don't just cite" pattern) during the
scaffolding pass, 2026-07-14; the `h2` correction and the additional
verification below during the crypto-core pass, same date.

Verification performed (crypto-core pass, 2026-07-14)
------------------------------------------------------

The pass that filled in every arithmetic body added the following
independently-computed test vectors and cross-checks (all wired into
the module's tests):

- **`G1`/`G2` scalar-multiplication cross-check vectors** (`[2]G`,
  `[k]G`, `[2]G2`, `[k]G2` compressed, for
  `k = 0x0123...cdef` — see `g1.zig`/`g2.zig`'s KAT comments): computed
  with an independent from-scratch big-integer implementation using
  TEXTBOOK AFFINE-coordinate formulas (chord-and-tangent with a modular
  inverse per operation) — deliberately a different algorithm family
  from the module's Jacobian/Montgomery arithmetic, so agreement is a
  genuine cross-check rather than a shared-bug echo.
- **`Fp6`/`Fp12` Frobenius coefficients**: computed PROGRAMMATICALLY in
  the module (`γ_1 = ξ^((p-1)/3)`, `γ = ξ^((p-1)/6)` via `Fp2.pow` with
  comptime-derived exponents — no transcribed table), then (a) verified
  DEFINITIONALLY by tests asserting `frobenius(a) == a^p` computed with
  nothing but `mul`/`square`, and (b) cross-checked against the same
  values independently recomputed with Python big-integer
  exponentiation — which also match the published
  `FROBENIUS_COEFF_FP6_C1[1]`/`FROBENIUS_COEFF_FP12_C1[1]` constants in
  zkcrypto/bls12_381 (constants compared as numbers; that source's CODE
  remains unread, per the Provenance section).
- **Quadratic-residue facts used by tests**: `x^3 + 4` is a QR mod `p`
  for `x = 4` (and NOT for `x = 1..3`); `ξ = 1 + u` is a quadratic
  non-residue in `Fp2` (`ξ^((p^2-1)/2) == -1`); the `G1` point with
  `x = 4` and the `G2` point with `x = u` are on-curve but NOT in the
  order-`r` subgroups (`[r]P != O`) — all computed independently with
  Python big-integer arithmetic.
- **`Fr.reduceWide` 64-byte vector**: `(2^512 - 1) mod r` computed
  independently and embedded as a byte-exact KAT.

Frobenius coefficients (resolution of "Deliberately NOT sourced")
------------------------------------------------------------------

The scaffold deliberately left the `Fp6`/`Fp12` Frobenius coefficients
un-hardcoded. The crypto-core pass resolved this with the scaffold's
recommended option (a): the coefficients are DERIVED IN CODE from the
verified `p` (comptime-derived exponents + runtime `Fp2.pow`) — no
hand-transcribed constant table exists anywhere in the module; the
tests above pin the derived values byte-exactly.

Verification performed (Part-2 crypto-core pass, 2026-07-14)
-------------------------------------------------------------

The pass that implemented the pairing added the following
independently-computed checks (all beyond the module's own
bilinearity property suite, which needs no external constant):

- **The hard-part exponent identity** (entry 17): verified as an EXACT
  integer equality (`k*(x+p)*(x^2+p^2-1) + 1 == (p^4-p^2+1)/r` for
  `x = -0xd201000000010000`) with independent Python big-integer
  arithmetic before implementation; the FCKRH chain (entry 14) was
  likewise verified to equal `3d` — the reason it was NOT used. Also
  verified numerically: `p^6 ≡ -1 (mod p^4-p^2+1)` (the
  conjugation-as-inversion fact the chain relies on), and `3 | (x-1)^2`
  (the comptime-enforced exactness of `k`).
- **The `e(G1,G2)` KAT** (entries 16 and 18): the IETF draft's
  Appendix B vector (primary, byte-for-byte) and py_ecc 8.0.0's
  independently computed value (cross-check, equal up to exactly one
  conjugation — the two projects' documented negative-seed-convention
  difference; the c0 halves are byte-identical and the c1 halves
  exactly negate mod p, both facts checked numerically). Two external
  sources plus this module's from-scratch third implementation agreeing
  (up to the documented convention) triangulate the value.
- **The `w^3` line-normalization fact** (`lineEval`'s doc comment —
  evaluating lines at the twisted image of `P` multiplies the Miller
  value by `w^3` per line, which final exponentiation kills):
  `(w^3)^((p^12-1)/r) = 1` follows from `(w^3)^(p^6-1) = -1` and
  `2 | (p^2+1)`; the byte-exact KAT above confirms the end-to-end
  normalization empirically.

**Status: Part 1 COMPLETE (crypto-core pass done, 2026-07-14).** All
field-tower and group arithmetic is implemented and KAT/property-tested.

**Status: Part 2 COMPLETE (crypto-core pass done, 2026-07-14 — see
entries 10-18 above for its design references).** The optimal ate
Miller loop, the full final exponentiation, `Fp12.cyclotomicSquare`
(Granger–Scott) and `Fp12.frobeniusMap` are implemented; the byte-exact
`e(G1,G2)` KAT (IETF draft primary + py_ecc conjugate cross-check) and
the full bilinearity property suite are wired and green.
`zig build test-bls12_381` passes 112/112 in Debug AND ReleaseFast.
See `SPEC.md`'s Status and Backlog.

Verification performed (Part-3 scaffold pass, 2026-07-14)
------------------------------------------------------------

- **RFC 9380's own text, not memory or a rendered page, was the
  source**: `rfc9380.txt` (entry 19) was fetched and every constant/
  vector this pass embeds — the K.1 `expand_message_xmd` vectors, the
  J.9.1/J.10.1 `u[0]`/`u[1]`/`P` values, and the `G1`/`G2` SSWU `Z`/
  `A'`/`B'` parameters (§8.8.1/§8.8.2) — was extracted from that raw
  text programmatically (a scratch Python parser, not part of this
  repository, same "compute independently, don't just cite" precedent
  as Parts 1-2's own verification), with every extracted field's byte
  length cross-checked against its expected width (48 bytes per `Fp`
  element, 96 for a `c0`/`c1` pair) before being embedded in
  `hash_to_curve.zig`.
- **`g1_iso_a`'s left-padding, caught and fixed by this cross-check**:
  RFC 9380's own text gives `A' = 0x144698a3...` — 94 hex digits (47
  bytes), one byte SHORTER than `Fp`'s 48-byte canonical width (a
  leading zero byte the RFC's own decimal-free hex notation omits, not
  an error in the RFC). Left-padded to `0x00144698a3...` (48 bytes)
  before embedding; `g1_iso_b` needed no padding (already exactly 96
  hex digits). Both re-verified structurally: `hexBytes(48, ...)` at
  comptime rejects any non-96-hex-digit-length input outright (compile
  error), so a hypothetical wrong-length transcription would have
  failed the build, not silently produced a wrong value.
- **The two REAL functions this pass adds
  (`expandMessageXmd`/`hashToFieldFp`/`hashToFieldFp2`) are pinned
  byte-exact against RFC 9380's own vectors, not merely believed
  correct by inspection**: `expandMessageXmd` against Appendix K.1 (all
  5 published messages, both published `len_in_bytes` values 0x20 and
  0x80 — the latter exercising `ell=4`, a case neither `frost`'s nor
  `voprf`'s existing fixed-`ell` `expand_message_xmd` implementations
  ever needed); `hashToFieldFp` against Appendix J.9.1's `u[0]`/`u[1]`
  (all 5 messages); `hashToFieldFp2` against Appendix J.10.1's
  `u[0]`/`u[1]` (`c0`/`c1` both checked, all 5 messages). All of these
  tests are RUN, not merely wired — `zig build test-bls12_381` reports
  them passing (see `SPEC.md`'s Verification section for the exact
  count).
- **The `Fp` wide-reduction construction** (`reduceWideToFp`,
  `hash_to_curve.zig`) was confirmed, not assumed, to be a valid
  generalization of `scalar.zig`'s existing `Fr.reduceWide`: by reading
  `std.crypto.ff`'s `Modulus(bits).reduce` signature directly
  (`fn reduce(self: Self, x: anytype) Fe` — generic in the input
  width), not by pattern-matching `Fr`'s code and hoping it transfers.

**Status: Part 3 SCAFFOLDED (2026-07-14).**
`expandMessageXmd`/`hashToFieldFp`/`hashToFieldFp2` were implemented
and byte-exact-KAT-verified against RFC 9380's own vectors (see
above); `sswuG1`/`isogenyMap11`/`sswuG2`/`isogenyMap3` were left as
`@panic("TODO(fable): ...")` stubs for the crypto-core pass below.

Verification performed (Part-3 crypto-core pass, 2026-07-14)
-------------------------------------------------------------

The pass that filled in the `map_to_curve` core (`sswuG1`/`sswuG2`,
`isogenyMap11`/`isogenyMap3`, plus the new `Fp.inv0`/`Fp.sgn0`/
`Fp2.inv0`/`Fp2.sgn0` helpers in `fp.zig`/`fp2.zig`) verified its
constants and construction as follows:

- **Isogeny coefficient tables (entries 20)**: parsed programmatically
  from `rfc9380.txt` (never retyped), then validated by an INDEPENDENT
  scratch Python implementation of the entire chain —
  `expand_message_xmd` → `hash_to_field` → §6.6.2 SSWU → Appendix
  E.2/E.3 `iso_map` (Horner over the parsed tables) → affine point add
  → `h_eff` scalar multiplication — using plain Python big-integer
  arithmetic and textbook affine group formulas (a different algorithm
  family from this module's `std.crypto.ff`/Jacobian arithmetic, so
  agreement is a genuine cross-check, same discipline as Parts 1-2).
  That independent implementation reproduces RFC 9380's OWN J.9.1 and
  J.10.1 vectors byte-exactly at every published stage (`u[0]`/`u[1]`,
  `Q0`/`Q1`, final `P`) for all 5 messages in both groups, where the
  J-vectors themselves were ALSO parsed from the raw text rather than
  retyped. Additionally, for arbitrary non-vector inputs, the parsed
  tables were confirmed to map points of `E1'`/`E2'` onto points of
  `E`/`E'` (curve-membership preservation — a whole-table structural
  check no single vector exercises). The module's own Zig tests then
  pin the same `Q0`/`Q1`/`P` vectors byte-exactly.
- **`h_eff` vs plain-cofactor divergence (entry 21)**: confirmed
  empirically with the independent implementation — for the RFC's own
  vector points `R = Q0 + Q1`, `[h1]R != [h_eff_G1]R` and
  `[h2]R != [h_eff_G2]R` in every case (both land in the order-`r`
  subgroup, but on DIFFERENT points), so using Part 1's
  `clearCofactor` here would silently break RFC 9380 suite conformance
  — exactly what §7's MUST NOT warns about. `hash_to_curve.zig`
  multiplies by `h_eff` via `scalarMulBytes` instead.
- **SSWU exceptional-branch totality**: verified numerically that
  `g(B/(Z*A))` is a quadratic residue on both `E1'` and `E2'` (so the
  `tv1 == 0` exceptional case never falls through to the gx2 branch),
  that `-1/Z` is a QR in `Fp` (both `u = 0` and `Z u^2 = -1` reach the
  G1 exceptional branch) but a non-residue in `Fp2` (only `u = 0`
  reaches G2's), and that the mapped points remain on `E1'`/`E2'` in
  all these cases; a Zig test pins the `u = 0` case for both groups.
- **Suite parameters re-confirmed**: the scaffold's embedded
  `A'`/`B'`/`Z` values for both suites were re-checked against the
  RFC's §8.8.1/§8.8.2 raw text during this pass (byte-for-byte,
  including `g1_iso_a`'s documented left-pad).

**Status: Part 3 COMPLETE (crypto-core pass, 2026-07-14).** The full
RFC 9380 chain is implemented and byte-exact against the RFC's own
vectors at every published stage; on-curve + subgroup property tests
cover non-vector messages. `zig build test-bls12_381` passes 128/128
in Debug AND ReleaseFast (0 panics); `zig fmt --check
modules/bls12_381/` is clean. See `SPEC.md`'s Status and Backlog.

