   status as `g1.zig`'s formula citations)**: the Bernstein–Lange
   Explicit-Formulas Database `shortw/jacobian-0` `add-2007-bl` /
   `madd-2007-bl` / `dbl-2009-l` formulas (the variable-time `G1`
   helpers — the `add-2007-bl` and `dbl-2009-l` shapes were already in
   `g1.zig` from Part 1; `madd-2007-bl` is its published `Z2 = 1`
   specialization); Pippenger's bucket method for `g1Msm` (textbook
   construction: windowed bucket accumulation + running-sum
   aggregation); the standard iterative radix-2 Cooley-Tukey butterfly
   network for the `Fr` NTT; and the Montgomery batch-inversion trick
   for `batchInvInPlace`. No third-party CODE was consulted, read, or
   ported for any of them.

**Status: Part 5 COMPLETE (2026-07-14).** Trusted-setup loader (with
process-wide memoization of the validated embedded setup — see
`SPEC.md`'s "Part 5 design"), wire codecs/validation, and the full KZG
crypto core are real; byte-exact against the `ethereum/c-kzg-4844`
vectors listed above and in the scaffold entry, in Debug and
ReleaseFast. See `SPEC.md`'s "Part 5 design" section and `kzg.zig`'s
own module doc comment.

Part 6 (threshold.zig) — SCAFFOLD + CRYPTO-CORE PASS (both 2026-07-14)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`threshold.zig` — trusted-dealer threshold BLS (Shamir secret sharing +
Feldman verifiable secret sharing + Lagrange-interpolation-in-the-
exponent combining), min-pk ciphersuite, built entirely on top of this
module's own Part 4 (`bls_sig.zig`) primitives. No external
specification is implemented wholesale here (unlike Parts 1-5, which
each pin a single named IETF/Ethereum spec): threshold BLS via Shamir +
Feldman VSS is textbook/folklore cryptographic construction — Shamir
("How to Share a Secret", CACM 1979) and Feldman ("A Practical Scheme
for Non-interactive Verifiable Secret Sharing", FOCS 1987) are the
named foundational references, cited for provenance/completeness (both
public, decades-old, uncopyrightable mathematical constructions — no
source of either was consulted or ported). The Lagrange-interpolation-
at-zero combining step, and the overall "Shamir-share the exponent,
Feldman-commit each coefficient, recombine linearly" shape, is the same
construction the sibling `frost` module already implements for
threshold Schnorr (RFC 9591 Appendix C, `frost.trustedDealerKeygen`/
`frost.secretShareCombine`) — `threshold.zig`'s constructions are
explicitly modeled after `frost.zig`'s ALREADY-REAL, KAT-validated
Horner-evaluation and Lagrange-interpolation loops, ported
conceptually (same algorithm shape, restated over this module's own
`Fr`/`G1`/`G2` types rather than `Secp256k1`) — no `frost.zig` CODE was
copied; `threshold.zig` does not `@import` `frost` and shares no types
with it. The non-interactive one-round signing/combining structure this
file exploits (no `frost`-style `SigningNonces`/binding-factor round
needed, because `bls_sig.sign`'s `R = SK * Q` equation is linear in
`SK`) is a well-known property of BLS in the threshold-signature
literature (e.g. Boldyreva, "Threshold Signatures, Multisignatures and
Blind Signatures Based on the Gap-Diffie-Hellman-Group Signature
Scheme", PKC 2003 — cited for provenance, not read as source for this
pass; the property itself follows directly from Part 4's own already-
implemented, already-cited `sign` equation).

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this
module.

**Status: Part 6 COMPLETE (scaffold + crypto-core pass, both
2026-07-14).** All functions are real: wire codecs, parameter
validation, `groupPublicKey`, the thin-over-`bls_sig` `partialSign`/
`verifyPartialSignature`, and the four genuinely cryptographic cores —
`evalPolynomialAt` (Shamir evaluation via Horner), `feldman
CommitCoefficient` (Feldman commit, `bls_sig.skToPk`'s construction),
`derivePublicKeyShare` (Feldman evaluate-in-the-exponent, Horner over
`G1`), and `combineSignatures`'s Lagrange-interpolation-in-the-exponent
step (`frost.deriveInterpolatingValue`'s exact coefficient formula over
this module's `Fr`). No external test vectors exist for the
trusted-dealer split itself (the coefficients are caller randomness);
the verification chain instead pins Part 6 transitively to REAL
vectors: the keystone tests assert that combining `t` partials equals
`bls_sig.sign(sk, msg)` BYTE-FOR-BYTE and verifies under the ordinary
`bls_sig.verify` — and Part 4's `sign`/`verify` are themselves pinned
to `ethereum/bls12-381-tests` v0.1.2 (see the Part 4 section above). A
full Pedersen/GJKR-style distributed key generation (no single trusted
dealer) is explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for this file — see `SPEC.md`'s
"Part 6 design"/"Out of scope for Part 6". See `SPEC.md`'s "Part 6
design" section and `threshold.zig`'s own module doc comment.
